#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/enums.md:1
msgid "Enums"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/enums.md:3
msgid ""
"Sometimes we need to handle multiple different types at runtime. Enums are a "
"powerful tool that allows us to safely and robustly describe situations like "
"this."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/enums.md:11
msgid "\"123\""
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/enums.md:12
msgid "\"[456, true, false]\""
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/enums.md:13
msgid "r#\"{ \"key\": \"value\" }\"#"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/enums.md:17
msgid "\"Parse the JSON string...\""
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/enums.md:32
msgid ""
"The other category of polymorphism is **dynamic polymorphism**, where we can "
"have different types of value at runtime, and we can't know statically which "
"type we'll have at any given time."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/enums.md:36
msgid ""
"As an example, consider parsing a JSON string. There are several different "
"types of JSON value, and which one we return depends on the contents of the "
"input string."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/enums.md:40
msgid ""
"Our `parse_json` function has to return a single, concrete type, and that "
"type needs to describe all of the possible types a JSON value can be. Enums "
"are a natural way of describing this kind of situation in Rust."
msgstr ""
